A||||5||My7Fieros||Is this a valid test for testing a possible clogged cat?||||2||
Z||000000||My7Fieros||02-10-2001||03:30 PM||||I saw some sort of Fieros online repair site or something on MSN while on a friends computer the other night (Im on AOL and didnt find that site under AOL search).<BR>Anyway, it said something to the effect of taking out the O2 sensor and plugging in some sort of vaccuum guage..says that if it reads over (some reading..I forget exactly) that the cat is clogged...anyone ever heard of this, or know of any poss way to see if the cat is clogged? I suspect I may have one in my 87 GT...thanx!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://tgowens.com/My7Fieros_sig.gif"> ||||reg||2
Z||000001||88formula||02-10-2001||04:34 PM||||The way to do it is to hook up a vacuum gauge to manifold vacuum and rev the engine up to about 5000 rpm. The vacuum of the engine should momentarily drop and then rebound to a higher reading than it was at idle. It should also rise as the engine rpm increases. If the vacuum actually drops or it takes a long time for the vacuum to rise when you snap the throttle open than you probably have an exhaust restriction.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||strdream||02-10-2001||07:22 PM||||7<BR>What you will find at the ox sensor will be pressure. There are kits for testing the cat where you drill a little hole before and after the cat, read and compare pressures. than mig weld the holes shut.<BR>One quick test is to unhook the cat or the ox sensor and test the ecceleration. you sould notice a big improvement if it is plugged. <BR>CAUTION must be taken to ensure that the exhaust discharge doesn't melt somthing or start a fire.<BR>Ross||||reg||1
Z||000003||strdream||02-10-2001||07:24 PM||||7<BR>What you will find at the ox sensor will be pressure. There are kits for testing the cat where you drill a little hole before and after the cat, read and compare pressures. than mig weld the holes shut.<BR>One quick test is to unhook the cat or the ox sensor and test the ecceleration. you should notice a big improvement if it is plugged. <BR>CAUTION must be taken to ensure that the exhaust discharge doesn't melt somthing or start a fire.<BR>Ross||||reg||1
Z||000004||daved||02-10-2001||10:26 PM||||I found it easier to remove the EGR valve (2 bolts)as it is in the exhaust system ahead of the Cat and muffler. It needed a new gasket anyway. <BR>I then made a small plate from 1/4" aluminum with a cheap 0-15 psi gauge screwed into a hole in the center. I didnt have a tap of the correct size, so just screwed the brass threads into an interference fit hole! Just bolted this where the EGR was, started the engine and revved up to see what pressure I had. Anything over say 1 or 2 PSI means the Cat or muffler is restricted.||||reg||1
Z||000005||oneof547||02-11-2001||11:29 PM||||A much more simple approach, which might night give you such exact results as a vacum test or such.Safely  Raise and support rear of car off ground far enough for you and a hammer to climb underneath and get to the cat converter. Tap cat moderately to hard with hammer in various spots, if you here any metal jingle around inside your cat is more than likely to be deteriorated enough to be hurting your mileage and performence considerable, not to mention making your motor run hotter than normal. Let me know what results u find.....<BR>One of 547<BR>88 Formula T-tops <P>------------------<BR>oneof547<p>[This message has been edited by oneof547 (edited 02-11-2001).]||||reg||1
